{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will re-criminalize possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, will allow individuals caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to enable law enforcement to more effectively address public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs in areas like parks.\n\n*** \nProponents argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents are concerned that this may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. The law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives but does not make it mandatory.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, individuals will be connected to a mobile behavioral health outreach team within 30 minutes if they meet the deflection criteria. As people across the country navigate this change, some may need to adjust their daily habits, much like deciding whether to eat at a local café or go shopping. The impact of this law will be felt across different age groups, and it's a topic that's on everyone's tongue. \nP.P.S As the country watches Oregon's new approach, it will be interesting to see how it influences policies elsewhere, potentially affecting how people live their lives.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.4740769863128662}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.0026958573143929243}, {"to